WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:08.596
A Torrance shopping center near bus service has a large surface parking lot, one-story buildings, and surrounding neighborhoods.

2
00:00:08.596 --> 00:00:12.643
It could remain unchanged, add homes and shops, or be rebuilt in phases.

3
00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:21.413
The planning challenge is to support useful infill without assuming that density, transit, affordability, and resilience will automatically arrive together.

4
00:00:21.413 --> 00:00:25.672
This episode is preparation for civic planning work, not legal advice.

5
00:00:25.672 --> 00:00:31.573
California housing, transportation, environmental, hazard, and permitting laws change frequently.

6
00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:41.003
Evaluate a real proposal using current statutes, state guidance, adopted local plans, site evidence, qualified technical analysis, and advice from counsel.

7
00:00:41.003 --> 00:00:44.349
Infill is development within an already urbanized area.

8
00:00:44.349 --> 00:00:53.232
It can reuse land and infrastructure, add housing near jobs and services, support transit, and reduce pressure to expand at the metropolitan edge.

9
00:00:53.232 --> 00:01:03.270
It can also strain aging infrastructure, displace residents or businesses, increase heat exposure, or place more people near pollution and hazards if poorly planned.

10
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.191
The word infill describes location, not quality.

11
00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:09.719
A car-oriented project on an urban parcel is still infill.

12
00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:13.674
A building near a bus stop is not necessarily transit-supportive.

13
00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:21.705
Resilient growth requires deliberate relationships among land use, mobility, infrastructure, affordability, ecology, and governance.

14
00:01:21.705 --> 00:01:23.591
Begin with location efficiency.

15
00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:30.709
Map jobs, schools, groceries, parks, health services, frequent transit, safe walking routes, and bicycle connections.

16
00:01:30.709 --> 00:01:32.960
Straight-line distance is not access.

17
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.435
A destination across a wide arterial without a safe crossing may be functionally far away.

18
00:01:38.435 --> 00:01:49.021
Transit-supportive development places enough residents, workers, and activities within practical reach of service, while designing the street and site to make transit useful.

19
00:01:49.021 --> 00:01:59.060
Density matters, but so do a connected network, active frontages, manageable blocks, safe crossings, shade, accessible paths, and limited delay for transit vehicles.

20
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.372
Transit frequency and span matter too.

21
00:02:01.372 --> 00:02:05.935
A stop with infrequent service that ends early may not support daily needs.

22
00:02:05.935 --> 00:02:13.053
Coordinate land use expectations with the transit operator’s service plans, capital constraints, and ridership goals.

23
00:02:13.053 --> 00:02:18.346
The city controls important pieces of the trip even when it does not operate the route.

24
00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:22.665
Mixing uses can shorten some trips and support activity across the day.

25
00:02:22.665 --> 00:02:28.080
It can also create operational conflicts involving deliveries, noise, odors, and parking.

26
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:36.841
Use site planning, building design, performance standards, and curb management to address real conflicts without defaulting to broad separation.

27
00:02:36.841 --> 00:02:38.829
Housing feasibility is essential.

28
00:02:38.829 --> 00:02:40.938
Planned capacity is not production.

29
00:02:40.938 --> 00:02:50.576
Parcel size, ownership, existing leases, construction cost, financing, remediation, fees, standards, and market conditions influence whether development occurs.

30
00:02:50.576 --> 00:02:55.577
Test prototype projects rather than assuming that a higher density label is enough.

31
00:02:55.577 --> 00:02:58.348
Affordable housing requires explicit strategy.

32
00:02:58.348 --> 00:03:05.878
Market-rate development may expand supply, but it does not by itself ensure homes affordable to people with very low incomes.

33
00:03:05.878 --> 00:03:16.602
Use the legally available combination of housing element programs, public funding, density bonus requirements, inclusionary policies, public land, preservation, and partnerships.

34
00:03:16.602 --> 00:03:20.337
Anti-displacement planning should begin before visible change.

35
00:03:20.337 --> 00:03:29.373
Map renters, lower-income households, cost-burdened residents, small businesses, culturally important places, and expiring affordability restrictions.

36
00:03:29.373 --> 00:03:33.289
Engage people without publicizing sensitive personal information.

37
00:03:33.289 --> 00:03:47.266
Possible responses include preservation funding, tenant protections allowed by law, affordable housing acquisition, relocation support, small-business assistance, phased construction, and right-to-return provisions where applicable.

38
00:03:47.266 --> 00:03:50.338
No single measure eliminates displacement pressure.

39
00:03:50.338 --> 00:03:56.001
Monitor both direct displacement from redevelopment and indirect pressure from changing costs.

40
00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:59.495
Infrastructure capacity must be tested at the right scale.

41
00:03:59.495 --> 00:04:08.290
Water, sewer, electricity, drainage, schools, parks, streets, and emergency services may have adequate systemwide capacity but a local bottleneck.

42
00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:12.447
Conversely, an old pipe does not prove that all growth is impossible.

43
00:04:12.447 --> 00:04:17.206
Identify the improvement, responsible entity, cost, timing, and funding method.

44
00:04:17.206 --> 00:04:23.833
Infill can improve fiscal efficiency by using existing systems, but older systems may need expensive upgrades.

45
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:26.966
Coordinate capital planning with development timing.

46
00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:31.303
Avoid requiring the first project to solve every accumulated deficiency.

47
00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:36.002
Avoid approving a sequence that leaves promised facilities permanently behind.

48
00:04:36.002 --> 00:04:38.231
Parking deserves contextual analysis.

49
00:04:38.231 --> 00:04:43.955
Large parking requirements consume land, raise cost, and can undermine transit-supportive form.

50
00:04:43.955 --> 00:04:46.967
Removing a minimum does not remove parking demand.

51
00:04:46.967 --> 00:04:55.461
Use occupancy data, shared parking, pricing, residential permits where justified, accessible parking, loading management, and travel options.

52
00:04:55.461 --> 00:05:01.100
Resilience asks whether growth can withstand, adapt to, and recover from stresses and shocks.

53
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:11.651
In Torrance, relevant analysis may include earthquakes, extreme heat, flooding, water constraints, power interruption, air quality, hazardous materials, and evacuation needs.

54
00:05:11.651 --> 00:05:15.774
Use current hazard information rather than copying an old checklist.

55
00:05:15.774 --> 00:05:19.958
Avoid placing more people in severe risk without a credible response.

56
00:05:19.958 --> 00:05:22.687
Sometimes the answer is to avoid development.

57
00:05:22.687 --> 00:05:30.872
Sometimes it is site remediation, structural design, cooling, backup power, floodable landscape, safer routes, or operational planning.

58
00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:35.663
The safety element and current LCI advisories should inform land use decisions.

59
00:05:35.663 --> 00:05:39.786
Climate resilience and emissions reduction are related but distinct.

60
00:05:39.786 --> 00:05:47.729
Infill near useful transit can reduce vehicle travel compared with more distant growth, especially when daily needs are accessible.

61
00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:52.701
A project must also address heat, energy, water, materials, and long-term hazards.

62
00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:55.491
Green infrastructure can perform several jobs.

63
00:05:55.491 --> 00:05:57.431
Trees provide shade and habitat.

64
00:05:57.431 --> 00:05:59.978
Landscaped systems can capture stormwater.

65
00:05:59.978 --> 00:06:04.040
Parks can offer recreation, cooling, and emergency gathering space.

66
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:09.194
These benefits require adequate soil, water, maintenance, and equitable distribution.

67
00:06:09.194 --> 00:06:11.377
Resilience includes social capacity.

68
00:06:11.377 --> 00:06:18.229
Neighbors, local organizations, trusted communication, affordable homes, and accessible services affect recovery.

69
00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:24.838
A technically resilient building in a community experiencing displacement may not represent resilient growth.

70
00:06:24.838 --> 00:06:27.324
Phasing is crucial on large infill sites.

71
00:06:27.324 --> 00:06:32.539
A shopping center may need to keep stores operating while homes and streets are built.

72
00:06:32.539 --> 00:06:39.937
Early phases should create usable connections and public benefits, not isolated buildings surrounded by temporary parking.

73
00:06:39.937 --> 00:06:47.213
Define interim conditions, construction routes, accessible paths, noise controls, and triggers for later infrastructure.

74
00:06:47.213 --> 00:06:50.063
Public-private coordination should be specific.

75
00:06:50.063 --> 00:06:57.278
A plan may identify a new street network, park, affordable housing commitment, transit facility, and stormwater system.

76
00:06:57.278 --> 00:07:00.977
Assign timing, funding, ownership, maintenance, and remedies.

77
00:07:00.977 --> 00:07:05.282
A concept map without implementation commitments is not a phasing plan.

78
00:07:05.282 --> 00:07:07.102
Measure performance over time.

79
00:07:07.102 --> 00:07:20.745
Track homes permitted and completed by affordability, business retention, transit ridership, walking access, vehicle travel, severe collisions, tree canopy, heat exposure, infrastructure delivery, and displacement indicators.

80
00:07:20.745 --> 00:07:23.473
Compare outcomes across groups and geography.

81
00:07:23.473 --> 00:07:26.141
Do not promise causation from one indicator.

82
00:07:26.141 --> 00:07:28.870
Ridership may change because service changed.

83
00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:32.265
Housing production may slow because interest rates rose.

84
00:07:32.265 --> 00:07:36.995
Monitoring should help leaders ask better questions and adjust implementation.

85
00:07:36.995 --> 00:07:39.299
Consider the Torrance shopping center.

86
00:07:39.299 --> 00:07:55.428
A resilient infill strategy could establish a connected street and path network, place buildings near sidewalks, phase homes with retail, reserve affordable housing sites, improve bus access, reduce unnecessary parking, add shaded public space, and upgrade drainage.

87
00:07:55.428 --> 00:08:06.403
It would also analyze nearby industrial activity, noise, hazardous materials, earthquake risk, utility capacity, school and park access, business leases, and household displacement.

88
00:08:06.403 --> 00:08:11.800
It would identify which improvements are required early and which depend on later phases.

89
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:19.076
The city should compare at least one lower-intensity and one higher-intensity alternative, plus a continuation scenario.

90
00:08:19.076 --> 00:08:26.413
Evaluate total capacity, feasibility, public benefits, infrastructure cost, transit support, hazard exposure, and equity.

91
00:08:26.413 --> 00:08:34.781
More density can support stronger transit and more housing, but only if standards, financing, and infrastructure make the capacity usable.

92
00:08:34.781 --> 00:08:37.989
The learner artifact is a resilient infill framework.

93
00:08:37.989 --> 00:08:42.167
Select one underused Torrance site without entering private property.

94
00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:45.375
Map a ten-minute walking area using actual crossings.

95
00:08:45.375 --> 00:08:52.518
List transit service, daily needs, infrastructure, hazards, existing residents or businesses, and missing connections.

96
00:08:52.518 --> 00:08:54.577
Propose a phased land use concept.

97
00:08:54.577 --> 00:09:03.718
Include an affordable housing action, anti-displacement action, transit action, resilience action, green infrastructure action, and funding dependency.

98
00:09:03.718 --> 00:09:07.653
Add five measures and two triggers that would change the phasing.

99
00:09:07.653 --> 00:09:09.045
Three recall questions.

100
00:09:09.045 --> 00:09:13.283
First, why does infill location not guarantee a good planning outcome?

101
00:09:13.283 --> 00:09:17.339
Second, what makes development transit-supportive beyond proximity?

102
00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:20.547
Third, why should anti-displacement work begin early?

103
00:09:20.547 --> 00:09:31.629
First answer: an infill project can still be inaccessible, unaffordable, hazardous, infrastructure-intensive, or car-oriented unless policy and design connect it to broader goals.

104
00:09:31.629 --> 00:09:42.587
Second answer: useful service, density and activity, safe and accessible routes, connected streets, supportive building form, and reliable transit operations must work together.

105
00:09:42.587 --> 00:09:53.050
Third answer: once acquisition, demolition, rent pressure, or business closure is underway, preservation and assistance options become more expensive and less effective.

106
00:09:53.050 --> 00:10:14.457
Source note: This original narration draws on the California Governor’s Office of Land Use and Climate Innovation 2017 General Plan Guidelines and current technical advisories concerning land use, housing, complete streets, SB 743 safety, environmental justice, fire hazards, evacuation, and climate resilience.

107
00:10:14.457 --> 00:10:21.271
The 2017 guidelines remain the current comprehensive edition while LCI prepares updated guidelines.

108
00:10:21.271 --> 00:10:27.535
Current California statutes and hazard information control over older educational material.

109
00:10:27.535 --> 00:10:45.431
The lesson also adapts infill, growth-management, implementation, and community-planning concepts from MIT OpenCourseWare, Community Growth and Land Use Planning, Fall 2010, together with public-action concepts from Urban Design Policy and Action, Spring 2007.

